"Connects paired numeric observations in a third ordering, usually time, to reveal a trajectory through value space.\n\nUse when: Two measured quantities change together and their sequence is important. Loops, reversals, or changing relationships matter more than separate time trends.\n\nAvoid when: Readers primarily need exact time alignment or the ordering is unavailable.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal and vertical positions encode the two measured quantities. Path order, arrows, or selected labels encode sequence through the observations.\n\nBuild: Place each paired observation on its correct numeric coordinates. Sort by the independent sequence field and connect consecutive records. Add sparse direction arrows and date labels outside paths at meaningful turning points.\n\nCheck: Check path order follows timestamps rather than either numeric axis. Verify gaps and duplicate times have explicit handling. Confirm arrows and annotations identify direction unambiguously.\n\nPitfalls: Self-crossings can encode returns to earlier value combinations; never alter coordinates to remove them. Prefer two aligned line charts by default when the path is hard to follow.\n\nExample: Trace synthetic monthly demand against capacity over two years, labeling January points and directional changes.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot, Line chart, Lag plot."
